**EXERCISE 1.23.** Suppose that the neighbourhood system $D$ is countable, say,

$$
D = \{X_0, X_1, X_2, \ldots, X_n, \ldots\}.
$$

Suppose further that the property of consistency of finite sequences of neighbourhoods is decidable (or "effectively known"). Then the following sequence is well defined:

$$
\begin{aligned}
Y_0 &= X_0 \\
Y_{n+1} &= X_{n+1}, \text{ if this set is consistent with } Y_0, Y_1, \ldots, Y_n; \\
&= Y_n, \text{ if not.}
\end{aligned}
$$

Show that $\{Y_0, Y_1, \ldots, Y_n, \ldots\}$ is a total element of $|D|$. (Hint: Show first that $Y_0, Y_1, \ldots, Y_{n-1}$ is consistent for all $n$.) In such a system show that all filters can be determined by sequences.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`Y`).** The greedy sequence that accepts the next enumerated neighbourhood precisely when it remains consistent with those already chosen.

**Definition (`greedyElement`).** The ideal element whose neighbourhoods are those containing some finite intersection of an initial segment of $Y$.

**Theorem (`Y_prefix_consistent`).** Every finite initial segment of $Y$ is consistent.

*Proof.* By construction each accepted step preserves a nonempty witnessing intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`greedyElement_isTotal`).** When the enumeration is surjective onto the neighbourhoods, the greedy element is total.

*Proof.* Any proper refinement would have to accept a neighbourhood rejected by greediness, contradicting consistency of the accumulated intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`filters_sequence_determined`).** Every ideal element is determined by a sequence of neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Enumerate those members of the enumeration that belong to the filter (or the master set otherwise). ∎

**Theorem (`exercise_1_23`).** The greedy sequence is finitely consistent; under a surjective enumeration the greedy element is total; and every filter is sequence-determined.

*Proof.* Assemble the three preceding theorems. ∎
